🎯 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

“The server is up, but the application is not accessible. How will you troubleshoot it?”

I would not immediately restart the server.

First, I want to understand where the request is failing.

I would start by checking whether the application process is running:

ps -ef | grep <app>

If it is managed by systemd:

systemctl status <service-name>

Next, I would check whether the application is listening on the expected port:

ss -lntp

For example, if the application should run on port 8080, I would verify that something is actually listening there.

Then I would test the application locally from the server:

curl -I http://localhost:8080

This is an important step.

If localhost works but users cannot access the application, I would investigate the network path.

I would check:

• Firewall rules
• AWS Security Groups / Network ACLs
• Load balancer health checks
• DNS resolution
• Reverse proxy configuration
• Network connectivity
• Recent deployments or configuration changes

I would also check application logs:

tail -f /var/log/<application>.log

or:

journalctl -u <service-name> --since "30 minutes ago"

Then I would compare the symptoms.

Server → Application → Port → Local connectivity → Network → Load Balancer → DNS → Client

If I identify the root cause, I would fix it according to the approved production procedure.

After the fix, I would verify the application from both the server and the user-facing endpoint.

Finally:

Issue → Investigation → Root Cause → Resolution → Preventive Action

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗧𝗮𝗸𝗲𝗮𝘄𝗮𝘆

“Server is up” does not mean “application is healthy.”

Good troubleshooting means following the request path and narrowing down exactly where the failure occurs.
